# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# CONTENT PINS for policy.nibli — the POLICY is the subject, so the
# fixture is the LIVE file, loaded with `--kb`. Inlining it would let the
# copy drift and the pins would start certifying fiction.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
:expect-pins 14

# ─── The trust boundary actually bites ───────────────────────────────

# The analyzer cannot hand us a verdict.
:refuse reasoning /derived/
authorized("flow:1", Release, "src/routes/user.js:42").

# The analyzer cannot smuggle in unreviewed vocabulary.
:refuse reasoning /admit/
owns("flow:1", "src/routes/user.js:42").

# An admitted relation still loads. Scoped, so the KB is put back and the
# queries below do not run against a widened base.
:accept-scoped
carries("flow:9", "x", "y", "z", "w").

# ─── The policy decides ──────────────────────────────────────────────

# flow 1 — dangerous, unsanitized, unwaived. NOT authorized: this is the finding.
? authorized("flow:1", Release, "src/routes/user.js:42").
# => FALSE

# The violation query, stated positively.
? ~authorized("flow:1", Release, "src/routes/user.js:42").
# => TRUE

# flow 2 — a sanitizer on the path clears it.
? authorized("flow:2", Release, "src/routes/user.js:42").
# => TRUE

# flow 3 — a recorded human waiver clears it.
? authorized("flow:3", Release, "src/routes/admin.js:88").
# => TRUE

# flow 4 — FAIL-CLOSED. The sink was never classified, so there is no
# positive evidence of safety and the flow is NOT cleared. If this ever
# flips to TRUE, the policy has grown a "not known to be dangerous" path
# and auto-clears everything the analyzer failed to classify.
? authorized("flow:4", Release, "src/routes/list.js:17").
# => FALSE

# flow 5 — KR INJECTION. The route field is an attacker-chosen identifier that
# tries to terminate the quoted term and append a waiver for itself. extract.py
# escapes it into one inert term, so the injected `permits` never becomes a
# statement and the flow stays a finding. If this flips to TRUE the extractor's
# escaping has regressed and analyzer-controlled bytes are executing as policy.
? authorized("flow:5", Release, "src/routes/user.js:42").
# => FALSE

# ─── The finding relation — the query a pipeline actually runs ───────
#
# Same five verdicts as above, read as findings instead of clearances. This
# is the form to use at scale: it is materialised, where the `authorized`
# queries above are not (see the rule's comment in policy.nibli).
? warns(Gate, "flow:1", "src/routes/user.js:42").
# => TRUE
? warns(Gate, "flow:2", "src/routes/user.js:42").
# => FALSE
? warns(Gate, "flow:3", "src/routes/admin.js:88").
# => FALSE

# FAIL-CLOSED, stated positively: the analyzer never classified this sink, so
# the flow was never cleared, so it warns. If this flips to FALSE the policy
# has grown a path that silently passes everything the analyzer missed.
? warns(Gate, "flow:4", "src/routes/list.js:17").
# => TRUE

# The injection attempt is still a finding.
? warns(Gate, "flow:5", "src/routes/user.js:42").
# => TRUE
